    {
      "id": "OuM4_8genwsfG4xNQg0cM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1140.334970139659,
      "y": 1446.6587411264134,
      "width": 796,
      "height": 1450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ar",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1799450125,
      "version": 2626,
      "versionNonce": 1509412141,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783615285946,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q-1. Is JavaScript a dynamically typed language or a statically type \nlanguage ?\n- JavaScript is a dynamically typed language.\n- When program executing that time check types.\n- So, we can just assign anything to the variable and it works fine.          \n- Typescript is a statically typed language. All checks are performed at\n   compile.\n\n* Javascript --> executing time        checktype\n* Typescript --> compile time  \n\nlet a\na = 0\nconsole.log(a)     -->  // 0\na = \"Hello\" \nconsole.log(a)     -->  // \"Hello\"\n\n\nQ-2. What are the different datatypes in javascript ? (Most asked)\n- Primitive datatypes :               - Non-Premitive datatypes :\n* string                                  * Object\n* number                                 * Array\n* boolen                                 * Date\n* null                                     * Function\n* undefined                             * Null\n* bigint                                  * Regular Expression\n* symbol\n\nQ-3. What is Hoisting in javascript ? (Most asked)\n- Hosting in javascript means that function and variable declaration are \nmoved to top of their scope before execution.\n- In simple word, you can access variable and function before execution,\nin var given undefine value but let, const given \"reference error\" let of\n\"temporal dead zone\".\n\nQ-4. What are the various things hoisted in javascript ?\n- Function declarations : fully hoisted.\n- Var : hoisted.\n- Arrow function : not hoisted.\n- Let : hoisted but not initialized. (Temporal dead zone)\n- Const : hoisted but not initialized. (Temporal dead zone)\n- Class declarations : hoisted but not initialized.\n\nRef : http://stackabuse.com/hoisting-in-javascript/\n\nQ-5. What is temporal dead zone ?\n- It is a specific time period in the execution of javascript code where the \nvariable declared with let and const exists but cannot be accesed untill the \nvalue is assigned.\n- Any attempt to access them result in reference errors.\n\n( in simple word variable declare with let, const and actual assign better between\ntime you can not access, if y try given \"refrence error\".)\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q-1. Is JavaScript a dynamically typed language or a statically type \nlanguage ?\n- JavaScript is a dynamically typed language.\n- When program executing that time check types.\n- So, we can just assign anything to the variable and it works fine.          \n- Typescript is a statically typed language. All checks are performed at\n   compile.\n\n* Javascript --> executing time        checktype\n* Typescript --> compile time  \n\nlet a\na = 0\nconsole.log(a)     -->  // 0\na = \"Hello\" \nconsole.log(a)     -->  // \"Hello\"\n\n\nQ-2. What are the different datatypes in javascript ? (Most asked)\n- Primitive datatypes :               - Non-Premitive datatypes :\n* string                                  * Object\n* number                                 * Array\n* boolen                                 * Date\n* null                                     * Function\n* undefined                             * Null\n* bigint                                  * Regular Expression\n* symbol\n\nQ-3. What is Hoisting in javascript ? (Most asked)\n- Hosting in javascript means that function and variable declaration are \nmoved to top of their scope before execution.\n- In simple word, you can access variable and function before execution,\nin var given undefine value but let, const given \"reference error\" let of\n\"temporal dead zone\".\n\nQ-4. What are the various things hoisted in javascript ?\n- Function declarations : fully hoisted.\n- Var : hoisted.\n- Arrow function : not hoisted.\n- Let : hoisted but not initialized. (Temporal dead zone)\n- Const : hoisted but not initialized. (Temporal dead zone)\n- Class declarations : hoisted but not initialized.\n\nRef : http://stackabuse.com/hoisting-in-javascript/\n\nQ-5. What is temporal dead zone ?\n- It is a specific time period in the execution of javascript code where the \nvariable declared with let and const exists but cannot be accesed untill the \nvalue is assigned.\n- Any attempt to access them result in reference errors.\n\n( in simple word variable declare with let, const and actual assign better between\ntime you can not access, if y try given \"refrence error\".)\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
